1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,330
What if a history lesson became a place you could investigate?

2
00:00:04,070 --> 00:00:05,770
This is Counterfactual Worlds.

3
00:00:07,370 --> 00:00:10,090
The teacher starts with a prepared Alexandria lesson.

4
00:00:10,890 --> 00:00:13,410
Students explore the city, then change one assumption.

5
00:00:13,930 --> 00:00:15,410
What if harbor trade collapsed?

6
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:20,380
A student predicts the library will definitely close.

7
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:23,480
Astra challenges that certainty and asks

8
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:24,660
what the evidence supports.

9
00:00:26,490 --> 00:00:28,030
The teacher adds a new challenge.

10
00:00:28,410 --> 00:00:30,190
Could another patron support the scholars?

11
00:00:30,910 --> 00:00:32,990
We preview the question, then add it

12
00:00:32,870 --> 00:00:34,210
to the same student world.

13
00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:40,500
Students read Strabo inside the app,

14
00:00:41,220 --> 00:00:43,560
distinguish historical evidence from invented teaching props,

15
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:44,800
and cite the source.

16
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:49,220
The revised explanation connects trade, income,

17
00:00:49,180 --> 00:00:51,240
and scholar support, while recognizing

18
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:53,500
that another patron could change the outcome.

19
00:00:56,430 --> 00:00:58,510
Counterfactual Worlds makes reasoning visible.
